"<strong>Self inductance.</strong> Ask Q8 first. The current ramps up, holds, ramps down and rests: ε is a constant −{#} mV on the way up, zero while I is steady (even at {#} A), and +{#} mV on the way down; the + and − marks on the coil show the voltage the source must supply. The U = ½LI² plot rises and falls back to zero, and the rail shows the source's work equal to Δ(½LI²). Then pick <strong>By hand</strong>: <strong>Ramp up</strong>, then <strong>Switch off</strong> ({#} ms): the spike is {#} times larger. Pick <strong>Triangle</strong> to show that ε follows the slope of I, not its sign. Try <strong>Toroid</strong> and a core μ_r; untick Blind to compare L with the formula and the end corrected (Nagaoka) value.",
"<strong>Q1.</strong> Predict: the north pole of a bar magnet is pushed toward a coil connected to a resistor. Does the coil attract or repel it? Which face of the coil acts as a north pole? What happens once the magnet stops inside the coil?",
